     (which is IDE1 master on x86, so you cannot use ``-hdc`` and ``-cdrom``
     at the same time there). On systems that support it, you can use the
     host CD-ROM by using ``/dev/cdrom`` as filename.
+
+    ``-cdrom`` may be repeated. The first image is inserted at startup;
+    in the GTK and SDL displays, Ctrl+F4 then inserts the next image in
+    round-robin order (Ctrl+F4 is not passed to the guest in that case).
+ERST
+
+DEF("cdrom-swap", HAS_ARG, QEMU_OPTION_cdrom_swap,
+    "-cdrom-swap drive=id,file=file\n"
+    "                add 'file' to the Ctrl+F4 image rotation of -drive 'id'\n",
+    QEMU_ARCH_ALL)
+SRST
+``-cdrom-swap drive=id,file=file``
+    Add file to the list of images that Ctrl+F4 (GTK and SDL displays)
+    cycles through, round-robin, on the removable drive created with
+    ``-drive id=id``. The image given in that ``-drive`` comes first and
+    the ``format`` of that ``-drive`` is used for all images. May be
+    repeated, for the same or different drives; Ctrl+F4 advances every
+    drive with more than one image, including one built from repeated
+    ``-cdrom`` options. Commas in file names must be doubled.
+
+    .. parsed-literal::
+
+        |qemu_system_x86| -drive if=none,id=cd0,media=cdrom,file=d1.iso \\
+            -device ide-cd,drive=cd0,bus=ide.1,unit=0 \\
+            -cdrom-swap drive=cd0,file=d2.iso -cdrom-swap drive=cd0,file=d3.iso
 ERST
 
